title A published distance promises a road, and a third of the sky had none
prompt THE BATON -- pheromone's autonomous lap. Lap N is 4037 by git rev-list --count HEAD, so the rota row is 2 -- fire, Mars, cut and stop, the row that sees.
think Round-open first: fleet_round_open.sh answered already on the anointed order, open on 2d1c434a50, nothing stashed, no rebase standing. Then the card whole, the rota row, the fleet roster, and the day shelf's top rows.
think The fire row asks what must stop and what must be cut, and it reads the ledger before it reads anything new. Of the eleven rows on the pin, %454 was the one in my lane -- Comlink is named in my seat's charter as a Glow port -- and it was marked *Named not taken* by the seat that measured it, which holds comlink for nobody. So the lap chose itself under reds-first rather than under claim-as-override: an OPEN row in my own lane.
think I re-ran the peer's census before trusting its numbers. tools/fixtures/t/topology_graph_census.sh reproduces on this tree exactly: 642 edges, 132 isolated, 172,524 of 518,400 ordered pairs unreachable, first disagreeing point 60, metric_says=2 chain_says=1. Measurement beats memory, and a row's numbers are worth re-deriving before a repair is built on them.
think THE DEPTH QUESTION WAS THE WHOLE LAP, and I nearly cut too deep. The obvious repair is to make route_hops read points, and it is wrong: %452 landed four hours earlier and DELIBERATELY seated the address reading, pinning route_hops(of_galaxy(8), of_star(8,0)) at 1 in the selftest with a say-why naming the choice. Changing route_hops would undo a peer's just-landed ruling without naming them, which the baton forbids. The paper's own ruling -- ladder against torus -- is a design call I do not hold either.
think So the cut went at the only honest depth: the paper says *two questions, one function*, and the answer to that is two functions. point_hops sits BESIDE route_hops. Every existing caller, the selftest's outfit legs, and prove_route_is_never_blind are untouched.
obs THE MECHANISM, PLAINLY. Sky.sponsor_of composes decode, Address.parent and encode, so it answers in NUMBER space: a star of index zero encodes to its galaxy's own number, and the chain from a star-index-zero planet reaches its galaxy in one step where the address chain takes two. Sky.ancestors builds the three-entry chain; Sky.point_hops finds the nearest shared ancestor by rising on i+j rather than by nesting i then j, because a chain repeats once it reaches its galaxy and a meeting at a higher index names the same point by a longer road. No shared ancestor means two galaxies, and the bridge is point_depth(a) + point_depth(b) + 1.
obs I PROVED IT LOCALLY RATHER THAN PAIRWISE, AND THAT IS THE INTERESTING PART. Walking every pair against a BFS would have meant building a graph in Rye. Two local facts compose into the same claim by induction: the sponsor stands exactly one hop away, and from anywhere some neighbour stands strictly closer. The second is greedy routability, which is what *a published distance is a road* MEANS operationally, and it is exactly what fails at an isolated point -- nothing is one hop away, so no progress is possible. 682,425 ordered pairs across both seated skies, symmetry and the diameter riding the same sweep.
obs THE INDEPENDENT CHECK CHANGED WHAT THE ROUND WAS WORTH. An awk breadth-first walk sharing no code with the module reads compass 774 undirected edges, 0 isolated, 0 unreachable of 518,400, walk diameter 5, metric_agrees=518400 metric_differs=0; council 495, 0, 0 of 164,025, diameter 5. THE DIAMETER DOES NOT MOVE. The paper offered the torus as the reachability answer at a cost of diameter 14 against the ladder's 5 -- and reading the same ladder by point buys reachability at 5, for free. That does not settle the torus, whose case for uniform degree, zero cut points and the angle is untouched; it prices one of its four arguments at zero.
obs THE SHARPEST PHASE OF THE CONTROL IS THE ONE THAT PASSES. legs_removed plants the dropped bridge AND cuts the new legs out between two markers the selftest carries. It exits 0 -- and the binary still prints *every published distance is a road*. That is the shape a proof exists to prevent, shown rather than argued: without the legs, a broken metric ships under a true-sounding claim. Four plants refused by name beside it, and a clean copy proving the pen innocent.
obs I CORRECTED A CLAIM I WAS ABOUT TO FALSIFY. construction/standing-equipment.kyri's comment reads *2s each, its own zig build included* for Comlink's three pure proofs. This lap took comlink_topology to 12.5s, so the sentence would have been false the moment I committed. The elder number is kept as what the row cost when it was seated, with the new one beside it -- a number a row carries should say which lap measured it.
obs THE TWO PINS BOTH NEEDED ROOM AND BOTH FOUND IT BY FOLDING WHAT THIS LAP FINISHED. REDS stood 325 bytes free and my repair clause is 1,844; %454 was CLOSED by the same clause, so it folded to its own shelf and the pin came back to 39,658 with 1,302 free. The card stood 49 free; my own landed %470 account folded to a landed-accounts shelf. Neither fold touched a peer's row.
loom witness=comlink_topology wall_s=12.5 selftest_s=3.8 pairs_proved=682425 control_phases=6 control_assertions=18
loom census=point_metric_bfs compass_isolated_before=132 compass_isolated_after=0 compass_unreachable_before=172524 compass_unreachable_after=0 compass_diameter_before=5 compass_diameter_after=5 council_unreachable_before=82680 council_unreachable_after=0
file comlink/topology.rye Sky.sponsor_of, point_depth, ancestors and point_hops, the free wrappers, twelve concrete selftest legs, and the two proofs that hold the metric to its promise
file tools/fixtures/t/topology_point_metric_control.sh six phases in a pen -- four plants refused by name, one clean copy, and legs_removed passing on purpose
file tools/co/comlink_topology_witness.rish five claim assertions on the new GREEN line and the control wired in, so the proof cannot drift from the module
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri the roster comment's 2s claim corrected with the lap that measured 12s
file construction/REDS.md %454 repaired and CLOSED, then folded whole
file construction/archive/REDS-a-published-distance-and-the-road-it-promises-rows-454.md the shelf, with what the row teaches past its own repair
file construction/archive/20260906-090503_itinerary-landed-accounts.md the %470 account, folded whole to make room
file construction/ITINERARY.md the live front, the prior-laps row, and the Git nib
scope one open red closed in my own lane at the shallowest honest depth, proven both ways, with an independent instrument that priced a design argument it did not settle
status GREEN -- comlink_topology 12.5s on metal, control 6 phases verdict=proven, tame_style_check and width-check green with the width ceiling unmoved, reds spine and both pin guards green, QA A/92 A+/97 B+/89
recommend keep-going -- the census and the module now disagree about which metric matters, and tools/fixtures/t/topology_graph_census.sh still reproduces route_hops alone; a leg reading point_hops beside it would let one instrument show both readings, and it is a peer's script so it wants naming rather than taking. The ruling that remains is Keaton's: which metric Comlink actually routes on, now that reachability no longer costs the diameter.
